  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · OEM</span>
    <h1>What is an OEM battery?</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Source: Chengguang Power Tech Co., Ltd. technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">An OEM battery is a battery produced to the buyer&rsquo;s specification and sold under the buyer&rsquo;s brand — rather than the factory&rsquo;s own label. The single sentence that matters is the one about ownership: you own the design and the brand, the factory owns the manufacturing.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> An OEM battery is built to your specification — voltage, Ah, CCA, terminal and label — and sold under your brand — a program that runs from RFQ to first container in 60–90 days. Quality is set by the specification you sign, not the label: the same factory often runs branded and OEM batteries on the same line, so the difference is whose specification and quality gate controls the output.
    </div>

    <h2>The core idea</h2>
    <p>OEM stands for original equipment manufacturer. In battery sourcing the term is used in reverse of how the car industry uses it: <em>you</em> are the buyer who owns the product definition and brand, and the factory is the original equipment manufacturer that produces to your agreed specification — voltage, capacity, CCA, standard family, case size, terminal layout and labelling.</p>

    <h2>OEM vs ODM vs white label: the ownership split</h2>
    <p>All three arrangements can look identical on a shelf — the difference is who owns the design, who owns the brand, and how much development risk you carry.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>Who owns design</th><th>Who owns brand</th><th>Typical buyer</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>OEM</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Buyer has a defined specification</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ODM</td><td>Manufacturer</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Buyer wants a proven platform</td></tr>
        <tr><td>White label</td><td>Manufacturer</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Buyer wants the fastest market entry</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><strong>OEM</strong> means you bring the specification and the manufacturer builds to it — you own the design. <strong>ODM</strong> means the manufacturer offers an existing design you can brand and adjust — they own the design, you own the brand. <strong>White label</strong> is the shallowest form: you apply your identity to an off-the-shelf product that any competitor can also buy. The deeper your ownership, the more control and the more development work. Read the <a href="/knowledge/oem-vs-odm-vs-private-label/">full OEM vs ODM vs private-label decision guide</a> before you choose, and see how it sits inside the broader <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-guide/">OEM manufacturing guide</a>.</p>

    <h2>What goes into an OEM battery specification</h2>
    <p>At minimum, an OEM battery project defines the standard family (JIS, DIN, BCI, AS or SA), voltage, capacity at C20 (Ah), CCA on a named test basis, dimensions, terminal type and polarity. Reference models such as 65D26 or H6 are often used as the starting point, then adjusted. The <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/">OEM specification guide</a> walks each parameter.</p>

    <h2>The advantages of OEM supply</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exclusivity</strong> — a product no competitor can buy off the shelf, because the specification is yours.</li>
      <li><strong>Consistency</strong> — a locked specification means the tenth container matches the first approved sample.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost control</strong> — you buy factory-direct capability, not a distributor's margin.</li>
      <li><strong>Brand building</strong> — the label, packaging and product are yours, so the equity accrues to you.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The risks, stated plainly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Specification drift</strong> — a repeat order that comes back different from what you approved. This is the classic failure mode, and it is prevented by a locked spec and a tested sample, not by trust.</li>
      <li><strong>Development effort</strong> — OEM puts the definition work on you. If you cannot specify CCA basis, terminal and group size precisely, you will pay for the factory to guess.</li>
      <li><strong>Minimums</strong> — a first order is typically one 20ft container (600–800 units), because weight caps the load before volume.</li>
      <li><strong>Lead-price exposure</strong> — lead is 75–80% of the material cost, so your price moves with the LME unless you negotiate a fixed-price clause.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why lead-acid OEM is a different game from lithium</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid is a 150-year-old supply chain with a mature, standardised product. Unlike lithium cells (Class 9), lead-acid starting batteries ship as Class 8 corrosive dangerous goods (UN 2794 / UN 2800) but need no UN 38.3 test report, so compliance is lighter. The chemistry is also a commodity: lead dominates the cost, so the factory's real value-add is process control and repeatability, not exotic technology — and lead's near-total recyclability is part of why the chemistry stays economically rational (see [Battery Council International's lead battery facts](https://batterycouncil.org/sources)). Read the <a href="/knowledge/how-lead-acid-batteries-are-made/">how lead-acid batteries are made</a> page to see why the manufacturing discipline matters more than the parts list.</p>

